1
00:00:01,083 --> 00:00:03,628
- "Previously on The Mentalist..."
- "Who's that?"

2
00:00:03,961 --> 00:00:06,130
Bob Kirkland, Homeland Security.

3
00:00:06,255 --> 00:00:08,341
- Do I know you?
- No, but I know you.

4
00:00:09,550 --> 00:00:10,676
Get me everything.

5
00:00:10,801 --> 00:00:13,054
"Jane made a lot of Red John
connections here."

6
00:00:13,179 --> 00:00:14,222
This is good.

7
00:00:14,347 --> 00:00:17,266
- "Why is this a Homeland Security issue?"
- "It's complicated."

8
00:00:22,813 --> 00:00:25,191
- Agent Lisbon, Jane, CBI.
- Sorry.

9
00:00:25,316 --> 00:00:26,609
F.B.I. Personnel only.

10
00:00:26,734 --> 00:00:29,570
We don't want to be here, either
but we were invited.

11
00:00:29,820 --> 00:00:32,698
Could you tell agent Reede Smith
that we're here as requested?

12
00:00:36,244 --> 00:00:37,745
What do you think
Smith wants?

13
00:00:38,246 --> 00:00:40,164
I don't know.
What do you think?

14
00:00:40,456 --> 00:00:43,376
Every time we go to see one of these
Red John suspects I get edgy.

15
00:00:43,501 --> 00:00:45,545
- Really? Can't tell.
- Really?

16
00:00:48,005 --> 00:00:50,007
Jane, Lisbon.
Thanks for coming.

17
00:00:50,883 --> 00:00:53,219
Miss this?
Who could say no?

18
00:00:55,137 --> 00:00:57,223
How can we be of help,
agent Smith?

19
00:00:57,723 --> 00:00:59,976
We caught a body.
I thought it might interest you.

20
00:01:00,476 --> 00:01:03,854
- And why would you say that?
- You tell me.

21
00:01:17,827 --> 00:01:20,955
- This is Benjamin Marx.
- The guy you buried alive.

22
00:01:21,080 --> 00:01:24,750
Excuse me.
The killer I buried alive.

23
00:01:25,668 --> 00:01:27,253
And I did let him out...
eventually.

24
00:01:28,337 --> 00:01:30,131
Not bad.
That's a tough I.D.

25
00:01:30,464 --> 00:01:32,508
considering most of his
face is burnt off.

26
00:01:33,759 --> 00:01:35,011
What do you think
happened to him?

27
00:01:35,136 --> 00:01:38,222
You're the F.B.I.
I'm sure you have the answers already.

28
00:01:39,599 --> 00:01:40,641
Okay.

29
00:01:41,183 --> 00:01:42,518
Tell me what I've come up with.

30
00:01:42,643 --> 00:01:46,022
Well, the first thing you'd notice
is that he was tortured...

31
00:01:46,522 --> 00:01:50,067
With fire and whatever it is people use
to cut someone's thumbs off.

32
00:01:50,192 --> 00:01:51,986
And what do I think
he was tortured for?

33
00:01:52,236 --> 00:01:55,031
Well, your guess would be
sadistic pleasure, but you'd be wrong

34
00:01:55,156 --> 00:01:57,325
more likely he was tortured
for information...

35
00:01:57,450 --> 00:01:59,035
Information that he didn't have.

36
00:01:59,201 --> 00:02:01,078
- How do you know he didn't have it?
- Because if he had it

37
00:02:01,203 --> 00:02:03,456
the killer would only have had
to burn off half his face.

38
00:02:05,249 --> 00:02:06,459
What else do I think?

39
00:02:07,001 --> 00:02:08,753
Well, whoever did the torturing
took their time

40
00:02:08,878 --> 00:02:12,256
so you would reason that they
had somewhere private to do it.

41
00:02:12,381 --> 00:02:14,300
- Right.
- And I'm sure you saw

42
00:02:14,425 --> 00:02:19,430
the sticky stuff on the leg of the victim
probably sent it off to the lab.

43
00:02:19,555 --> 00:02:22,433
And in three days they'll tell you
that it's pine sap, which makes sense

44
00:02:22,558 --> 00:02:25,561
because there's a broken pine needle
wedged into the sole of his shoe.

45
00:02:25,686 --> 00:02:28,147
- Yeah, I see.
- Eventually you'd come to the conclusion

46
00:02:28,272 --> 00:02:30,441
that he was kept
in the woods someplace

47
00:02:30,816 --> 00:02:32,818
before he was killed
and dumped here.

48
00:02:33,527 --> 00:02:34,695
Nice.

49
00:02:35,988 --> 00:02:37,573
Why are we here, agent Smith?

50
00:02:38,532 --> 00:02:40,534
Marx was out on bail
while he stood trial.

51
00:02:41,285 --> 00:02:42,912
Things were looking good
for him, too.

52
00:02:43,412 --> 00:02:45,539
I mean, you tortured him into a confession

53
00:02:45,665 --> 00:02:48,876
so a little good lawyering
a little luck, and he could walk.

54
00:02:49,710 --> 00:02:52,004
Bet that really chapped
your hide, huh, Jane?

55
00:02:52,129 --> 00:02:53,172
Not at all.

56
00:02:53,589 --> 00:02:56,634
That's the best theory
the F.B.I. can come up with?

57
00:02:56,759 --> 00:02:59,929
That Jane killed Benjamin Marx
and burned his face off?

58
00:03:00,846 --> 00:03:02,431
We're exploring every angle.

59
00:03:03,307 --> 00:03:04,350
He's an angle.

60
00:03:04,475 --> 00:03:07,436
Listen. If I wanted
to kill Benjamin Marx

61
00:03:07,561 --> 00:03:09,271
I would have done it
the first time.

62
00:03:09,522 --> 00:03:12,233
He was a sociopath that stole
from widows and orphans

63
00:03:12,358 --> 00:03:14,026
and he got what he deserved.

64
00:03:14,902 --> 00:03:16,237
But I didn't do it.

65
00:03:17,613 --> 00:03:19,448
- We'll see.
- Yes, we will.

66
00:03:21,200 --> 00:03:22,535
Thanks for coming.

67
00:03:25,496 --> 00:03:27,123
- What's going on, Jane?
- Nothing.

68
00:03:27,248 --> 00:03:29,166
- Tell me.
- I said it's nothing.

69
00:03:29,291 --> 00:03:31,085
It's never nothing with you.

70
00:03:31,627 --> 00:03:34,672
- Do you think Smith is Red John?
- Possibly.

71
00:03:34,797 --> 00:03:36,590
Well, if he isn't Red John,
he's an idiot

72
00:03:36,716 --> 00:03:38,676
for thinking you could have anything to do
with Marx's death.

73
00:03:38,801 --> 00:03:39,927
He's not completely wrong.

74
00:03:40,052 --> 00:03:44,265
I mean, I didn't kill Benjamin Marx
but I might have gotten him killed.

75
00:03:57,501 --> 00:04:00,045
Okay, no more secrets.
Spill it now.

76
00:04:00,921 --> 00:04:04,633
I was concerned with security when I was
working my list of Red John suspects.

77
00:04:04,758 --> 00:04:06,844
- Obsessively secretive.
- Yeah, well, you say "tomato"

78
00:04:06,969 --> 00:04:09,763
but I knew there were people
that needed that information.

79
00:04:09,888 --> 00:04:15,018
So I planted what you would call
misinformation on my crime board.

80
00:04:15,143 --> 00:04:18,230
So whoever saw your crime board got
the list of the seven Red John suspects.

81
00:04:18,355 --> 00:04:21,191
{\an8}But they would be the wrong suspects.
Exactly. And it worked.

82
00:04:21,316 --> 00:04:23,735
{\an8}- Someone broke into my office.
- How do you know?

83
00:04:23,861 --> 00:04:25,946
{\an8}Well, I set a trap,
and it was sprung.

84
00:04:26,405 --> 00:04:29,366
{\an8}Now this person has a fake list
of Red John suspects

85
00:04:29,491 --> 00:04:31,910
{\an8}and Benjamin Marx
was on that list.

86
00:04:32,327 --> 00:04:36,373
{\an8}So somebody is torturing and killing
the people on your fake list.

87
00:04:36,498 --> 00:04:38,333
{\an8}Whoa, whoa.
Not necessarily.

88
00:04:38,458 --> 00:04:41,086
{\an8}Someone tortured and killed
one person from my list.

89
00:04:41,211 --> 00:04:44,423
{\an8}But theoretically, his death
could be about anything.

90
00:04:44,840 --> 00:04:46,425
{\an8}He wasn't a nice man.

91
00:04:47,843 --> 00:04:50,345
{\an8}I am trying to wrap
my mind around this.

92
00:04:50,470 --> 00:04:53,265
{\an8}You put the lives of seven
innocent people in danger.

93
00:04:53,390 --> 00:04:54,766
{\an8}No. None of them are innocent.

94
00:04:55,058 --> 00:04:56,894
{\an8}Marx defrauded
hundreds of people...

95
00:04:57,019 --> 00:04:58,770
{\an8}then he brutally murdered
someone to cover it up.

96
00:04:58,896 --> 00:05:00,314
{\an8}I assure you, Lisbon...

97
00:05:00,731 --> 00:05:03,066
{\an8}all of these men
are very bad.

98
00:05:03,358 --> 00:05:04,985
{\an8}They just don't happen
to be Red John.

99
00:05:09,907 --> 00:05:12,326
{\an8}Do you think the real Red John
could have broken in here

100
00:05:12,451 --> 00:05:13,827
{\an8}and killed Marx?

101
00:05:13,952 --> 00:05:17,331
{\an8}Well, Red John is the only person besides
us that knows the real list of suspects

102
00:05:17,456 --> 00:05:20,375
{\an8}so he is the only person that
has no motive to do this.

103
00:05:21,209 --> 00:05:24,421
{\an8}- Then who broke in here?
- I don't know.

104
00:05:24,671 --> 00:05:27,716
{\an8}Someone with easy access,
a lot of resources and power.

105
00:05:28,175 --> 00:05:29,301
{\an8}The F.B.I.

106
00:05:29,426 --> 00:05:31,887
{\an8}they've been wanting to get their hands
on the Red John case for a while.

107
00:05:32,012 --> 00:05:34,723
{\an8}They were not happy when
Homeland Security took over.

108
00:05:34,848 --> 00:05:36,266
True.

109
00:05:36,391 --> 00:05:40,479
{\an8}You know, I've always suspected
the F.B.I. are watching me. Personally.

110
00:05:40,854 --> 00:05:45,192
{\an8}As paranoid as it sounds it would explain
how they got the Marx case.

111
00:05:45,400 --> 00:05:48,028
{\an8}But why would they do this to him?
And how do we proceed?

112
00:05:48,153 --> 00:05:50,781
{\an8}I can't just go to the F.B.I. and ask them
if they killed Marx.

113
00:05:51,615 --> 00:05:53,617
{\an8}If the F.B.I. has been
watching me

114
00:05:53,742 --> 00:05:56,244
{\an8}I'll bet that someone
very high up in the CBI

115
00:05:56,370 --> 00:05:58,455
{\an8}authorized it,
or at least went along with it.

116
00:05:58,914 --> 00:06:01,416
{\an8}- I need to talk to them.
- Bertram.

117
00:06:01,541 --> 00:06:03,126
{\an8}But you obviously can't ask him.

118
00:06:03,251 --> 00:06:04,920
Madeleine Hightower then.

119
00:06:05,212 --> 00:06:07,631
{\an8}Hightower? She hasn't run
the CBI in three years.

120
00:06:07,756 --> 00:06:10,592
{\an8}What kind of information do you
think she still has access to?

121
00:06:11,009 --> 00:06:12,970
{\an8}And if so, why would she
share it with you?

122
00:06:13,095 --> 00:06:14,763
Well, Red John tried to kill her.

123
00:06:14,888 --> 00:06:17,516
- She's gonna want to help me.
- Unless she blames you.

124
00:06:19,977 --> 00:06:22,270
- I'm gonna call her.
- Fine.

125
00:06:22,729 --> 00:06:24,439
{\an8}Oh. What do you have
planned for the day?

126
00:06:24,564 --> 00:06:27,567
{\an8}You don't officially have a case,
do you? You can relax.

127
00:06:27,943 --> 00:06:30,779
Get a massage,
mani/pedi.

128
00:06:31,446 --> 00:06:32,489
No.

129
00:06:32,614 --> 00:06:34,282
I have to go
and offer protection

130
00:06:34,408 --> 00:06:37,244
to the six people on your list,
which you are going to give me.

131
00:06:37,995 --> 00:06:40,288
I was hoping
you'd opt for the massage.

132
00:06:44,126 --> 00:06:45,794
Oh, I can't believe it.

133
00:06:46,461 --> 00:06:47,921
Finally got him back to sleep.

134
00:06:48,964 --> 00:06:50,632
Good job, Mr. Rigsby.

135
00:06:51,508 --> 00:06:53,885
And we don't have to leave for work
for another hour.

136
00:06:54,469 --> 00:06:56,221
What are we gonna do
to pass the time?

137
00:06:56,346 --> 00:06:58,932
Well, I don't know,
Mrs. Rigsby.

138
00:07:04,062 --> 00:07:06,606
- Oh... Don't.
- Oh...

139
00:07:09,568 --> 00:07:12,195
- It's Lisbon.
- She can wait 10 minutes.

140
00:07:20,245 --> 00:07:22,664
- Lisbon.
- It must be important. You should get it.

141
00:07:24,708 --> 00:07:26,168
Morning, boss.

142
00:07:27,252 --> 00:07:29,713
You and Rigsby take these four.
I'll take Haibach and Lee.

143
00:07:29,838 --> 00:07:31,339
Isn't Haibach the guy
from the panzer case

144
00:07:31,465 --> 00:07:32,799
with all the photos
of the little girls?

145
00:07:32,924 --> 00:07:34,176
Yeah. I've dealt
with him once before

146
00:07:34,301 --> 00:07:36,428
so maybe he'll be more likely
to come with me.

147
00:07:36,553 --> 00:07:38,388
Rape charges, drug charges

148
00:07:38,805 --> 00:07:40,432
human trafficking charges.

149
00:07:40,557 --> 00:07:41,933
Really?
We're gonna help these guys?

150
00:07:42,059 --> 00:07:44,561
That's the job.
We don't get to pick and choose.

151
00:07:44,728 --> 00:07:47,022
We need to warn these men
that their lives are in danger

152
00:07:47,147 --> 00:07:48,565
and offer them protection.

153
00:07:49,483 --> 00:07:50,776
Now get on it.

154
00:07:54,446 --> 00:07:56,615
And I was watching her
brush her teeth this morning

155
00:07:56,740 --> 00:07:59,242
and she does the full two minutes
just like the dentist says.

156
00:07:59,618 --> 00:08:01,286
The wrong person,
that could be really annoying

157
00:08:01,411 --> 00:08:03,705
but today I just thought, cool.

158
00:08:03,830 --> 00:08:06,625
I'm gonna be spending the rest of my life
with those really good teeth, you know?

159
00:08:07,042 --> 00:08:08,085
Nope.

160
00:08:08,210 --> 00:08:11,213
My point is, marriage is
even better than I thought.

161
00:08:11,338 --> 00:08:12,380
Except...

162
00:08:13,423 --> 00:08:16,593
well... it's not a big deal.
It's only been three days.

163
00:08:16,718 --> 00:08:18,720
It's sort of funny, really.

164
00:08:19,387 --> 00:08:21,014
We haven't done it yet.

165
00:08:22,099 --> 00:08:23,391
As a married couple.

166
00:08:23,767 --> 00:08:26,520
What about at the hotel?
After the wedding. I got you that carriage.

167
00:08:26,728 --> 00:08:28,105
Yeah, we passed out.

168
00:08:28,230 --> 00:08:30,148
It was a very emotionally
draining day.

169
00:08:31,024 --> 00:08:32,776
- Plus we drank too much.
- Right.

170
00:08:32,901 --> 00:08:34,319
Then we had to work
the next day.

171
00:08:34,444 --> 00:08:35,821
And since then, Sara's
at a conference in L.A.

172
00:08:35,946 --> 00:08:38,323
so we've had ben every day,
morning and night.

173
00:08:38,448 --> 00:08:39,616
You know, I read
a study on the internet.

174
00:08:39,741 --> 00:08:42,452
It said women lose interest in sex
almost immediately after marriage.

175
00:08:42,577 --> 00:08:44,663
- It's Darwinian.
- Nah, not Grace.

176
00:08:44,788 --> 00:08:46,248
She's always been super into...

177
00:08:47,124 --> 00:08:49,126
My point is, I don't believe it.
It's not true.

178
00:08:49,876 --> 00:08:51,461
I'm just telling you
what the internet said.

179
00:09:00,887 --> 00:09:02,681
Rick Dort? CBI.
Open up.

180
00:09:03,014 --> 00:09:05,725
Anyone can say anything on the internet.
Doesn't mean it's true.

181
00:09:08,228 --> 00:09:09,604
- Yeah?
- Mr. Dort?

182
00:09:09,729 --> 00:09:12,607
Agents Rigsby and Cho from the CBI.
We want to talk to you about...

183
00:09:31,168 --> 00:09:32,836
Hands in the air,
or I'll shoot you in the head.

184
00:09:33,503 --> 00:09:34,671
Hey, man, don't shoot.

185
00:09:34,796 --> 00:09:36,631
We have reason to believe
your life's in danger.

186
00:09:36,756 --> 00:09:38,258
We came to offer
police protection.

187
00:09:38,717 --> 00:09:41,136
- What?
- Your life is in danger.

188
00:09:41,511 --> 00:09:42,762
It is?

189
00:09:44,514 --> 00:09:47,100
You're not here about
the meth... other thing?

190
00:09:47,559 --> 00:09:48,894
The meh... other thing?

191
00:09:50,395 --> 00:09:51,730
There is no other thing.

192
00:09:52,272 --> 00:09:54,733
- Slip of the tongue.
- Get out of the pool.

193
00:09:59,571 --> 00:10:01,865
Hey, Grace.
You look a little peaked.

194
00:10:02,115 --> 00:10:04,993
I found out why you haven't been able
to get in touch with Hightower.

195
00:10:05,493 --> 00:10:07,871
- What is it? Tell me.
- I spoke to her aunt

196
00:10:07,996 --> 00:10:10,540
and she said Hightower
and the kids had an accident.

197
00:10:11,291 --> 00:10:14,753
- They're dead.
- What? When?

198
00:10:15,003 --> 00:10:18,548
Two weeks ago on vacation
in Mexico. They drowned.

199
00:10:18,673 --> 00:10:20,342
How come we didn't hear
about it sooner?

200
00:10:21,092 --> 00:10:22,344
It's very strange.

201
00:10:22,469 --> 00:10:24,387
Apparently, they never
found the bodies

202
00:10:24,512 --> 00:10:27,015
and authorities have called off
the search and rescue.

203
00:10:30,101 --> 00:10:32,062
What did you say
her aunt's name was?

204
00:10:32,187 --> 00:10:34,272
Ruby. Ruby Hightower.

205
00:10:34,522 --> 00:10:36,399
Yeah, well, I'm gonna
need an address.

206
00:10:37,067 --> 00:10:38,818
I need to send her some flowers.

207
00:10:40,528 --> 00:10:41,738
That's terrible.

208
00:10:43,740 --> 00:10:45,533
- "This is Bob Kirkland."
- Kirkland.

209
00:10:45,992 --> 00:10:48,119
This is agent Reede Smith, F.B.I.

210
00:10:48,245 --> 00:10:49,454
What can I do for you, Smith?

211
00:10:49,913 --> 00:10:51,331
"I think it's what I can do for you."

212
00:10:52,415 --> 00:10:55,001
My superiors asked that I fill you in
on a case I'm working.

213
00:10:56,127 --> 00:10:58,713
- It involves Patrick Jane.
- I'm listening.

214
00:10:59,714 --> 00:11:01,091
"It's a murder/kidnapping thing."

215
00:11:01,216 --> 00:11:04,135
"Vic's a guy named Benjamin Marx.
Jane dealt with him before."

216
00:11:04,594 --> 00:11:07,097
Used some... shall we say

217
00:11:07,222 --> 00:11:09,557
over-the-top techniques
to get Marx to confess?

218
00:11:09,891 --> 00:11:11,393
That sounds about right.

219
00:11:11,935 --> 00:11:13,853
"Marx was on trial,
could have gone free."

220
00:11:14,688 --> 00:11:16,147
Frankly, I like Jane for it.

221
00:11:16,648 --> 00:11:18,441
He's got a vigilante beef
with Red John.

222
00:11:18,942 --> 00:11:20,151
Maybe this is his thing.

223
00:11:20,277 --> 00:11:21,945
I think you might be on to something.

224
00:11:22,654 --> 00:11:24,281
- Nice work.
- "Thanks."

225
00:11:24,572 --> 00:11:28,076
- And, Smith? Keep me in the loop.
- Yes, sir.

226
00:11:37,210 --> 00:11:39,462
- Yes?
- Mrs. Hightower?

227
00:11:40,463 --> 00:11:42,173
I'm so sorry for your loss.

228
00:11:42,299 --> 00:11:44,050
I used to work with
your niece Madeleine

229
00:11:44,175 --> 00:11:47,971
and, uh, well, I was hoping
that maybe we could talk.

230
00:11:48,305 --> 00:11:49,472
These are for you.

231
00:11:50,515 --> 00:11:51,975
Oh, please come in.

232
00:11:53,393 --> 00:11:55,478
Thank you. They're very nice.

233
00:11:57,230 --> 00:11:59,566
When was the last time
you saw your niece?

234
00:12:00,275 --> 00:12:03,028
A few weeks ago, right before
they went on vacation.

235
00:12:04,112 --> 00:12:06,156
They made a point
to come and see me.

236
00:12:07,532 --> 00:12:09,909
At least I got a chance
to say good-bye.

237
00:12:10,035 --> 00:12:12,120
Yeah, well, there...
there's that.

238
00:12:17,375 --> 00:12:20,503
- What is it?
- Oh, it's just, uh...

239
00:12:25,133 --> 00:12:27,260
I just wish that there was
some way I could...

240
00:12:29,679 --> 00:12:31,264
Let her know
how I feel.

241
00:12:31,765 --> 00:12:32,932
There is.

242
00:12:33,308 --> 00:12:35,769
My neighbor is very good
with the computer.

243
00:12:36,227 --> 00:12:39,564
He set up a private memorial site
on the internet

244
00:12:39,689 --> 00:12:41,107
just for friends and family.

245
00:12:42,025 --> 00:12:43,318
I'd love to see that.

246
00:12:47,113 --> 00:12:49,783
- Okay, here you go.
- Oh, do you mind?

247
00:12:49,908 --> 00:12:51,701
I'm not very good
with computers.

248
00:12:51,826 --> 00:12:53,912
Sure. Just say
what you need to say.

249
00:12:55,080 --> 00:12:56,122
Write this.

250
00:12:57,415 --> 00:12:58,625
In death...

251
00:13:00,251 --> 00:13:01,920
you have found new life...

252
00:13:04,089 --> 00:13:06,383
and I want to shout it
from the mountaintops.

253
00:13:09,469 --> 00:13:11,721
I know in my heart

254
00:13:12,931 --> 00:13:14,474
dearest Madeleine...

255
00:13:16,017 --> 00:13:17,685
that when sunset comes

256
00:13:18,478 --> 00:13:20,647
I will find you
waiting for me...

257
00:13:21,314 --> 00:13:23,024
in the heavenly gardens.

258
00:13:25,485 --> 00:13:26,653
Love, Patrick.

259
00:13:29,239 --> 00:13:31,950
Oh... That was, um...

260
00:13:32,075 --> 00:13:33,201
that's lovely.

261
00:13:34,828 --> 00:13:36,162
Very poetical.

262
00:13:36,746 --> 00:13:39,124
Thank you.
Feels a lot better.

263
00:13:41,042 --> 00:13:44,462
- I should... I should go.
- Oh, you poor lamb.

264
00:13:44,963 --> 00:13:47,257
You were a little sweet
on her, weren't you?

265
00:14:32,010 --> 00:14:34,762
No. I remember you.
Talk to my lawyer.

266
00:14:35,221 --> 00:14:36,347
Mr. Haibach.

267
00:14:36,473 --> 00:14:38,683
It's not what you think.
Just hear me out.

268
00:14:40,226 --> 00:14:42,812
I think you've wanted to pin
something on me for a long time.

269
00:14:42,937 --> 00:14:46,232
- I won't be your patsy, Ms. Lisbon.
- It's agent Lisbon.

270
00:14:46,357 --> 00:14:48,735
And we have reason to believe
that your life is in danger.

271
00:14:48,860 --> 00:14:50,820
I am here to offer you
police protection.

272
00:14:50,945 --> 00:14:53,198
So if I go with you,
nothing bad will happen to me?

273
00:14:54,073 --> 00:14:56,868
Sounds like police state
trickery, agent Lisbon.

274
00:14:56,993 --> 00:14:58,578
The Stasi would be proud.

275
00:14:58,703 --> 00:15:01,039
I am legally and morally bound
to inform you that...

276
00:15:01,164 --> 00:15:03,750
Please don't try to stake out
the moral high ground here.

277
00:15:03,875 --> 00:15:06,920
If you had your way, I'd be in jail
for a crime I didn't commit.

278
00:15:07,045 --> 00:15:08,588
I think I'll take my chances.

279
00:15:10,298 --> 00:15:11,549
"How's it going, Jane?"

280
00:15:11,674 --> 00:15:15,512
- I just visited with Hightower's aunt.
- "That's so sad."

281
00:15:15,637 --> 00:15:16,763
Did you find anything out?

282
00:15:16,888 --> 00:15:18,723
I mean, do you think Red John
had anything to do with it.

283
00:15:18,848 --> 00:15:21,100
I don't think so.
Not his M.O.

284
00:15:21,768 --> 00:15:24,562
- So you think it's just an accident?
- Looks like it.

285
00:15:24,687 --> 00:15:25,855
How's the roundup going?

286
00:15:25,980 --> 00:15:30,193
Haibach wouldn't come with me.
I think he's hiding something.

287
00:15:30,318 --> 00:15:33,279
Well, a guy like that
probably takes issue with lady police.

288
00:15:33,988 --> 00:15:35,323
"Ladies in general."

289
00:15:35,448 --> 00:15:38,076
Uh, you're right. You know what?
I'll send Cho and Rigsby.

290
00:15:38,201 --> 00:15:41,538
Or not. Would the world really miss him
if something happened?

291
00:15:41,871 --> 00:15:44,290
And that's the difference
between you and me.

292
00:15:44,415 --> 00:15:45,458
"And good-bye."

293
00:15:52,590 --> 00:15:54,342
If you are from CBI, I'm...

294
00:16:19,348 --> 00:16:20,391
What happened?

295
00:16:20,892 --> 00:16:23,311
We found the door open
blood and beer bottle on the floor.

296
00:16:23,436 --> 00:16:25,605
No Haibach.
Rigsby's canvassing the neighbors.

297
00:16:30,067 --> 00:16:31,152
This is bad.

298
00:16:34,614 --> 00:16:35,781
Not really.

299
00:16:36,115 --> 00:16:39,619
Confirms the theory that someone is going
through my list trying to find Red John.

300
00:16:39,744 --> 00:16:42,330
Haibach has been abducted
and he's probably gonna be tortured to death.

301
00:16:42,830 --> 00:16:44,373
Yeah, he's in for a rough night.

302
00:16:44,499 --> 00:16:46,209
And we have to tell Smith
and the F.B.I.

303
00:16:46,334 --> 00:16:49,378
that he's been taken and that
it's connected to the Marx case.

304
00:16:49,504 --> 00:16:51,255
- No, we don't.
- We can't lie.

305
00:16:52,298 --> 00:16:53,841
We do it all the time.

306
00:16:53,966 --> 00:16:56,093
He's gonna put it together eventually
and it'll be worse.

307
00:16:56,219 --> 00:16:59,764
Okay, fine. Tell him.
But do not mention the list.

308
00:16:59,889 --> 00:17:02,934
Just tell him that someone else connected
to an old case of ours has gone missing.

309
00:17:03,059 --> 00:17:04,101
Agreed.

310
00:17:04,852 --> 00:17:05,937
What do we do now?

311
00:17:07,438 --> 00:17:09,273
I will have the answer
for that very soon.

312
00:17:17,490 --> 00:17:19,242
I have black, milk...

313
00:17:20,243 --> 00:17:21,702
and milk and sugar.

314
00:17:21,827 --> 00:17:23,579
I'll take whatever
you don't want.

315
00:17:23,704 --> 00:17:26,415
- You always this thorough?
- I like to cover all my bases.

316
00:17:27,875 --> 00:17:29,001
Milk and sugar.

317
00:17:31,337 --> 00:17:32,421
Thanks.

318
00:17:36,300 --> 00:17:37,802
So you said you got
something for me?

319
00:17:39,345 --> 00:17:42,765
There's been an interesting development
in that case I was telling you about.

320
00:17:44,141 --> 00:17:47,311
Looks like another man's gone missing
that's connected with Jane.

321
00:17:48,271 --> 00:17:51,649
- A Richard Haibach.
- That is interesting.

322
00:17:52,858 --> 00:17:53,901
Thank you.

323
00:17:55,361 --> 00:17:57,196
Look, it's none
of my business, but...

324
00:17:57,863 --> 00:17:59,782
can I ask what the deal is
with you and Jane?

325
00:18:01,242 --> 00:18:03,494
- Long story.
- Whatever.

326
00:18:04,245 --> 00:18:05,663
I'm not looking to pry.

327
00:18:07,999 --> 00:18:09,333
I like that.

328
00:18:10,751 --> 00:18:12,295
I've done some research on you.

329
00:18:14,005 --> 00:18:16,424
- On me?
- Yeah. You're a good agent.

330
00:18:17,091 --> 00:18:18,426
Real straight shooter.

331
00:18:19,218 --> 00:18:20,761
Why would you research me?

332
00:18:23,931 --> 00:18:26,267
I have some other business
I wanna discuss with you.

333
00:18:28,019 --> 00:18:33,190
- Delicate business.
- Okay. Shoot.

334
00:18:35,276 --> 00:18:36,611
Tiger tiger.

335
00:18:41,741 --> 00:18:43,200
Tiger tiger.

336
00:18:44,619 --> 00:18:47,204
What, are you messing with me?
What... what is that? "Tiger tiger"?

337
00:18:48,331 --> 00:18:51,500
- You really don't know?
- Know what?

338
00:18:54,587 --> 00:18:55,880
Nothing. That's a test.

339
00:18:56,964 --> 00:18:58,007
Okay.

340
00:18:58,716 --> 00:19:00,968
I'm totally in the dark here.
You want to fill me in?

341
00:19:04,180 --> 00:19:08,184
While investigating Red John
Homeland Security's begun to suspect

342
00:19:08,351 --> 00:19:11,937
the existence of a powerful
secret organization

343
00:19:12,063 --> 00:19:14,565
within California
law enforcement.

344
00:19:14,774 --> 00:19:17,985
Police, CBI, D.O.J.

345
00:19:18,486 --> 00:19:20,363
Strictly high-level, we think.

346
00:19:20,821 --> 00:19:22,865
- What do they do?
- They do dirt.

347
00:19:24,075 --> 00:19:26,327
They protect each other
with major cover-ups

348
00:19:27,328 --> 00:19:28,579
get away with murder.

349
00:19:30,331 --> 00:19:31,707
What's the tiger thing?

350
00:19:32,625 --> 00:19:34,085
We think that's the password.

351
00:19:35,294 --> 00:19:36,420
Spooky.

352
00:19:37,004 --> 00:19:39,632
- Is Red John a member?
- It's too soon to tell.

353
00:19:40,466 --> 00:19:41,759
What about Jane?

354
00:19:43,761 --> 00:19:45,680
We're just starting
to put this thing together.

355
00:19:46,305 --> 00:19:48,391
We're looking for partners
within the F.B.I.

356
00:19:50,559 --> 00:19:53,896
lower level guys that...
we're sure we can trust.

357
00:19:56,315 --> 00:19:58,943
That's me.
Happy to help.

358
00:19:59,276 --> 00:20:00,444
Thank you.

359
00:20:01,946 --> 00:20:03,322
I'll be in touch.

360
00:20:07,034 --> 00:20:08,494
Thanks for the coffee.

361
00:20:20,005 --> 00:20:21,132
Hello, Patrick.

362
00:20:22,466 --> 00:20:23,676
Madeleine.

363
00:20:24,677 --> 00:20:27,138
Thank you for coming.
Tea?

364
00:20:28,472 --> 00:20:29,640
Yes, please.

365
00:20:37,982 --> 00:20:39,358
Just so we're clear

366
00:20:40,401 --> 00:20:42,403
I have a gun pointed
under the table.

367
00:20:46,157 --> 00:20:48,284
And it's pointed
at something important.

368
00:20:49,910 --> 00:20:51,036
Fair enough.

369
00:20:54,874 --> 00:20:56,959
- How are the kids?
- They're safe.

370
00:20:57,835 --> 00:20:59,420
That's all you need to know.

371
00:21:01,630 --> 00:21:03,966
Your aunt is a delight.

372
00:21:04,675 --> 00:21:05,843
Wonderful actress.

373
00:21:06,719 --> 00:21:09,305
I had to do what was best for me
and my kids.

374
00:21:10,097 --> 00:21:13,350
I knew that Red John was cleaning house
after Lorelei Martins was killed.

375
00:21:13,476 --> 00:21:16,103
- I did not want to be next.
- No, I got it.

376
00:21:16,771 --> 00:21:18,063
He's already tried
to kill you once.

377
00:21:18,189 --> 00:21:19,857
It's not something you ever
really get over.

378
00:21:19,982 --> 00:21:22,526
So what's so damn important
you had to bring me out of hiding?

379
00:21:22,735 --> 00:21:25,696
I'm getting close to catching Red John
and I need your help.

380
00:21:27,281 --> 00:21:29,700
I'm not putting my family in danger
to help you.

381
00:21:30,409 --> 00:21:33,037
Besides, I've been out of law enforcement
for three years.

382
00:21:33,537 --> 00:21:35,498
How can I help you?
I don't know anything.

383
00:21:35,623 --> 00:21:36,749
Really?

384
00:21:39,293 --> 00:21:44,215
Because I think you've been holding
something back for a very long time.

385
00:21:45,007 --> 00:21:47,426
Yeah, there it is.
I'm right.

386
00:21:49,678 --> 00:21:51,055
The F.B.I....

387
00:21:52,139 --> 00:21:53,808
They've been watching me,
haven't they?

388
00:21:55,726 --> 00:21:58,729
Please, I wouldn't be here
if it weren't important.

389
00:22:00,022 --> 00:22:01,148
Tell me.

390
00:22:02,233 --> 00:22:04,485
Well, I guess there's no reason
not to tell you now.

391
00:22:05,945 --> 00:22:08,405
But obviously you did not
hear this from me.

392
00:22:17,832 --> 00:22:19,834
When Minnelli was chief
of the CBI

393
00:22:20,918 --> 00:22:23,337
He struck a deal with F.B.I.
Director Schultz.

394
00:22:23,462 --> 00:22:26,131
- What did Schultz want?
- She wanted reports.

395
00:22:27,174 --> 00:22:28,217
On you.

396
00:22:28,759 --> 00:22:30,344
Minnelli provided them.

397
00:22:30,928 --> 00:22:33,764
When Minnelli retired,
that became a part of my job.

398
00:22:34,431 --> 00:22:37,142
But the intel wasn't exactly
going to the F.B.I.

399
00:22:38,185 --> 00:22:41,522
I found out that Schultz was passing it on
to Homeland Security.

400
00:22:41,814 --> 00:22:43,607
- Bob Kirkland?
- Yes.

401
00:22:44,316 --> 00:22:46,610
- Why?
- He thought you were Red John.

402
00:22:48,320 --> 00:22:49,572
Of course.

403
00:22:51,824 --> 00:22:54,118
How long have Homeland Security
been watching me?

404
00:22:54,660 --> 00:22:56,078
Eight, nine years.

405
00:22:58,122 --> 00:22:59,999
I did what I thought was best at the time.

406
00:23:00,124 --> 00:23:02,668
- Don't expect me to apologize.
- No, no, I won't.

407
00:23:03,085 --> 00:23:04,128
Jane.

408
00:23:05,129 --> 00:23:09,049
You cannot tell anyone you saw me.
Even Lisbon.

409
00:23:09,174 --> 00:23:12,303
And I won't.
I meant what I said.

410
00:23:12,803 --> 00:23:17,224
I'm gonna kill Red John
and you're gonna get your life back.

411
00:23:17,349 --> 00:23:18,642
I hope so.

412
00:23:20,519 --> 00:23:21,979
So what's next?

413
00:23:22,771 --> 00:23:24,481
Well, I'm gonna go see
Bob Kirkland.

414
00:23:26,275 --> 00:23:30,779
What I'm looking for is an open
and honest dialogue.

415
00:23:32,406 --> 00:23:34,241
100% transparency.

416
00:23:35,242 --> 00:23:37,995
I don't know what you want from me.
I didn't do anything wrong.

417
00:23:38,996 --> 00:23:40,372
Maybe not yet.

418
00:23:40,581 --> 00:23:43,250
But... That room
in your basement.

419
00:23:44,126 --> 00:23:47,755
Come on.
You are a depraved man.

420
00:23:48,505 --> 00:23:50,174
At minimum, a pedophile.

421
00:23:51,592 --> 00:23:53,636
But frankly, that's not
why we're here.

422
00:23:55,262 --> 00:24:00,351
Why then?
Please just tell me... Why.

423
00:24:01,810 --> 00:24:03,896
I brought you here
to ask you some questions.

424
00:24:05,522 --> 00:24:07,358
Anything. I'll...I'll tell you anything.

425
00:24:08,025 --> 00:24:10,152
All right then.
Tell me...

426
00:24:10,444 --> 00:24:12,780
Do you recognize me?
Have you seen me before?

427
00:24:13,197 --> 00:24:14,990
No... I don't think so.

428
00:24:15,616 --> 00:24:17,743
Okay, we'll come back to that.
Next question.

429
00:24:17,868 --> 00:24:18,911
What?

430
00:24:19,828 --> 00:24:20,871
What?

431
00:24:22,373 --> 00:24:25,376
- Are you Red John?
- What?

432
00:24:25,501 --> 00:24:27,461
Are you... Red John?

433
00:24:30,172 --> 00:24:31,465
No...

434
00:24:32,257 --> 00:24:35,094
- Are you Red John?!
- No!

435
00:24:44,851 --> 00:24:46,645
Agent Kirkland? Hey...

436
00:24:47,771 --> 00:24:50,357
- Jane, what are you doing here?
- Can I have a quick word?

437
00:24:50,482 --> 00:24:52,275
It's nothing private.
Your friends can stay.

438
00:24:52,776 --> 00:24:53,986
I'll catch up, guys.

439
00:25:00,200 --> 00:25:05,122
I have a pet theory
I was hoping you could confirm or Deny.

440
00:25:06,832 --> 00:25:08,125
I'll see what I can do.

441
00:25:08,667 --> 00:25:11,003
Has Homeland Security been
investigating me?

442
00:25:11,586 --> 00:25:12,713
Yes.

443
00:25:13,213 --> 00:25:15,090
I'm surprised you're
allowed to tell me that.

444
00:25:15,215 --> 00:25:17,467
I mean, you guys are usually
so cloak and dagger.

445
00:25:17,884 --> 00:25:21,013
Well, it's no secret that Homeland Security
has the Red John case.

446
00:25:21,680 --> 00:25:23,724
Yeah, your name has come up
in various ways.

447
00:25:23,849 --> 00:25:27,728
Right. So then did you guys
break into my office

448
00:25:27,853 --> 00:25:30,981
- and steal my list of suspects?
- Absolutely not.

449
00:25:31,106 --> 00:25:32,357
No? Why not?

450
00:25:32,482 --> 00:25:34,985
Because we don't care
who you think Red John is.

451
00:25:35,110 --> 00:25:36,403
We have the case.

452
00:25:37,279 --> 00:25:38,447
Point taken.

453
00:25:39,823 --> 00:25:41,033
Thanks for your time.

454
00:25:41,825 --> 00:25:42,951
That's it?

455
00:25:43,618 --> 00:25:46,329
Yeah. I just wanted to make sure
you weren't on a wild goose chase

456
00:25:46,455 --> 00:25:47,998
because whoever it was
that broke into my office

457
00:25:48,123 --> 00:25:50,042
they stole a fake list
of suspects.

458
00:25:54,504 --> 00:25:57,007
- Well played.
- Thank you. I thought so.

459
00:25:58,842 --> 00:26:02,012
- Maybe you should come work for us.
- Maybe not.

460
00:26:17,527 --> 00:26:19,112
You need a ride somewhere?

461
00:26:21,281 --> 00:26:23,492
No, I'm...I'm good, thanks.

462
00:26:24,076 --> 00:26:26,286
Well, we should talk some more.
Get in the car.

463
00:26:30,707 --> 00:26:33,293
- Do I have a choice?
- No.

464
00:26:35,003 --> 00:26:36,296
By all means...

465
00:26:37,798 --> 00:26:39,299
Let's talk some more.

466
00:26:45,430 --> 00:26:46,681
Let me see your phone.

467
00:26:46,932 --> 00:26:49,810
Hey, you don't have to do this.
I actually want to talk to you.

468
00:26:50,060 --> 00:26:51,603
Just hand me the phone.

469
00:27:09,496 --> 00:27:10,956
Seatbelt, please.

470
00:27:15,544 --> 00:27:18,380
- Hey, have you guys heard from Jane?
- Nope.

471
00:27:18,505 --> 00:27:20,382
I haven't seen him
since yesterday morning.

472
00:27:20,507 --> 00:27:22,384
His phone just keeps on
going straight to voicemail.

473
00:27:22,509 --> 00:27:23,718
Can you ping it for me?

474
00:27:24,177 --> 00:27:27,681
- Sure. It'll take me a little while.
- Thanks. It's probably nothing.

475
00:27:34,771 --> 00:27:36,273
And here we are.

476
00:27:40,068 --> 00:27:41,319
Keep moving.

477
00:27:51,204 --> 00:27:53,290
Oh, I love what you've done
with the place.

478
00:27:55,250 --> 00:27:57,169
Come on, I'll give you
the 10-cent tour.

479
00:28:11,474 --> 00:28:13,476
Help me... Please.

480
00:28:13,602 --> 00:28:17,022
- Richard Haibach, Patrick Jane.
- Help me.

481
00:28:17,689 --> 00:28:21,443
I just wanted you to see the face of the man
who got you into this mess.

482
00:28:21,568 --> 00:28:25,197
It's a funny story, actually...
A comedy of errors.

483
00:28:25,322 --> 00:28:26,615
Please let me go.

484
00:28:26,740 --> 00:28:28,074
- No.
- Please.

485
00:28:28,200 --> 00:28:31,411
But don't worry.
Jane'll get his, too.

486
00:28:39,544 --> 00:28:43,381
So, let's talk about that real list
of Red John suspects.

487
00:28:47,135 --> 00:28:50,472
I couldn't get a hit on Jane's phone.
Wherever he is, he has no cell service.

488
00:28:52,140 --> 00:28:53,600
That's probably him now.

489
00:28:55,060 --> 00:28:56,353
This is Lisbon.

490
00:28:56,478 --> 00:28:59,272
"Hello, Lisbon.
Do you know who this is?"

491
00:28:59,773 --> 00:29:00,815
Yes.

492
00:29:01,900 --> 00:29:03,109
I thought you were dead.

493
00:29:04,694 --> 00:29:07,739
- I met with Jane yesterday.
- He didn't say.

494
00:29:07,864 --> 00:29:09,574
"I asked him not to tell you"

495
00:29:10,158 --> 00:29:12,285
but I think I might have
gotten him into trouble.

496
00:29:12,911 --> 00:29:15,872
- I've been trying to call him all day.
- Me, too.

497
00:29:15,997 --> 00:29:17,749
"I gave him some
information yesterday."

498
00:29:17,874 --> 00:29:20,460
Did he say anything to you
about who he was going to see?

499
00:29:20,585 --> 00:29:22,587
- No. Should he have?
- "Yes."

500
00:29:23,755 --> 00:29:24,965
"We need to talk."

501
00:29:26,383 --> 00:29:29,344
I think I might have caused Jane
to do something very dumb.

502
00:29:33,932 --> 00:29:38,853
Funny thing about the list.
I'd... Rather not share it with you.

503
00:29:39,604 --> 00:29:42,983
I get it. It's been your pet project
for a long time.

504
00:29:43,650 --> 00:29:44,693
Mine, too.

505
00:29:46,152 --> 00:29:47,654
But you will share it with me.

506
00:29:51,700 --> 00:29:53,618
What if I just give you
one name to start?

507
00:29:56,913 --> 00:29:58,456
Robert Kirkland.

508
00:30:00,292 --> 00:30:01,626
Interesting.

509
00:30:03,253 --> 00:30:04,671
Makes sense, actually.

510
00:30:05,505 --> 00:30:08,133
But it doesn't really help me
with my cause, though, does it?

511
00:30:08,258 --> 00:30:11,094
No, I suppose not.
I was wrong about you.

512
00:30:11,219 --> 00:30:13,555
You're not Red John.
You're hunting him, same as me.

513
00:30:13,680 --> 00:30:15,640
Not exactly the same.

514
00:30:15,765 --> 00:30:19,269
I'm willing to kill seven people
to get the job done.

515
00:30:19,394 --> 00:30:21,354
- Touché.
- Why aren't you?

516
00:30:21,688 --> 00:30:23,273
'Cause that would
make me a murderer.

517
00:30:23,398 --> 00:30:26,234
You're planning on murdering Red John.
That doesn't make any sense.

518
00:30:26,359 --> 00:30:28,445
Okay, so we...we both have
different approaches

519
00:30:28,570 --> 00:30:31,781
but the important thing here
is that we share a common goal.

520
00:30:31,906 --> 00:30:34,159
We can help each other
if we work together.

521
00:30:34,284 --> 00:30:38,079
I'm not interested in working together
and I doubt you are, either.

522
00:30:40,290 --> 00:30:43,543
We both want our revenge
and only one of us is going to get it.

523
00:30:48,256 --> 00:30:49,591
Robert Kirkland.

524
00:30:50,800 --> 00:30:54,971
- I know I should be surprised, but...
- But he's a creepy guy, that's why.

525
00:30:55,764 --> 00:30:56,931
Where would he take Jane?

526
00:30:57,057 --> 00:30:59,392
Probably the same place
he took Benjamin Marx.

527
00:30:59,517 --> 00:31:01,895
Jane saw a pine needle
in Marx's shoe.

528
00:31:02,020 --> 00:31:04,981
It was like he'd walked through
a pine forest before he was killed.

529
00:31:05,106 --> 00:31:07,150
Lots of pine needles
in northern California.

530
00:31:07,275 --> 00:31:09,069
Kirkland would be
keeping up appearances.

531
00:31:09,194 --> 00:31:11,571
He'd be going to the office
every day, so...

532
00:31:12,155 --> 00:31:14,407
pine forest within a hundred-mile
radius of Sacramento?

533
00:31:14,532 --> 00:31:16,368
Sure, sure.
If that's all we got.

534
00:31:19,913 --> 00:31:20,955
"Hey, boss."

535
00:31:21,081 --> 00:31:23,041
Van Pelt, I need you
to run something for me.

536
00:31:23,166 --> 00:31:25,502
Properties or businesses
owned by Robert Kirkland

537
00:31:25,627 --> 00:31:29,089
"within 100 miles of the capitol...
in close proximity to a pine forest."

538
00:31:29,214 --> 00:31:31,966
- Robert Kirkland?
- It's a long story.

539
00:31:32,842 --> 00:31:35,178
Nothing for Robert Kirkland
within a hundred miles.

540
00:31:37,263 --> 00:31:39,307
But there's a Michael Kirkland.

541
00:31:39,599 --> 00:31:42,227
Owns a cabin up near
El Dorado state park.

542
00:31:42,352 --> 00:31:44,896
- Oh, is he a relative?
- I don't know. I can check.

543
00:31:45,021 --> 00:31:46,981
- All right, text me the address.
- "Sure."

544
00:31:47,524 --> 00:31:49,109
We should call local
authorities for backup.

545
00:31:49,234 --> 00:31:51,111
No. No. No cops.

546
00:31:51,236 --> 00:31:53,196
Nobody can know about me.
Please.

547
00:31:53,405 --> 00:31:56,491
Okay. How do we do this?

548
00:32:06,209 --> 00:32:07,460
That's how.

549
00:32:10,296 --> 00:32:13,675
- Do you have siblings, Jane?
- No.

550
00:32:16,761 --> 00:32:18,888
I had a twin brother...
Michael.

551
00:32:19,722 --> 00:32:21,349
We were very close
growing up.

552
00:32:21,474 --> 00:32:23,268
I hear that's a strong bond.

553
00:32:25,311 --> 00:32:27,981
And our father was
a sadistic drunk.

554
00:32:28,106 --> 00:32:30,442
He beat the crap out of us
and our mother.

555
00:32:31,484 --> 00:32:33,736
Beat her till she couldn't
take it anymore.

556
00:32:33,862 --> 00:32:35,947
- She left?
- With a razor blade.

557
00:32:38,575 --> 00:32:41,369
After that
it was just me and Michael.

558
00:32:42,412 --> 00:32:43,955
I was all he had.

559
00:32:44,080 --> 00:32:46,082
- You were the stronger one.
- Yeah.

560
00:32:46,749 --> 00:32:50,295
I grew up. I got away.
But Michael couldn't do it.

561
00:32:51,588 --> 00:32:56,801
He stayed at home and watched
our father drink himself to death.

562
00:32:57,177 --> 00:32:59,137
The apple doesn't fall
far from the tree.

563
00:33:00,013 --> 00:33:01,097
You're right.

564
00:33:01,222 --> 00:33:06,394
Michael turned to the bottle
drank like his life depended on it.

565
00:33:06,895 --> 00:33:08,688
I paid for treatments.

566
00:33:09,147 --> 00:33:10,523
I took him to meetings.

567
00:33:11,232 --> 00:33:13,818
- Nothing worked.
- Until?

568
00:33:14,360 --> 00:33:18,615
Until one day he met someone
a man who could help him.

569
00:33:18,740 --> 00:33:20,825
More than a man.
A savior.

570
00:33:20,950 --> 00:33:23,119
Yeah. Red John.

571
00:33:24,496 --> 00:33:27,832
He only asked for a few
simple things in return.

572
00:33:27,957 --> 00:33:30,293
Devotion and sacrifice.

573
00:33:31,169 --> 00:33:33,546
Michael drifted
further and further away

574
00:33:33,671 --> 00:33:37,550
until one day he was gone.
I never saw him again.

575
00:33:38,009 --> 00:33:39,719
You think Red John
killed your brother.

576
00:33:41,679 --> 00:33:42,847
Yes.

577
00:33:44,224 --> 00:33:47,894
I never found him
but in my heart, I know.

578
00:33:53,691 --> 00:33:57,570
Look at me blab.
I... You're not my therapist.

579
00:33:58,071 --> 00:34:00,156
I didn't bring you here to talk.

580
00:34:01,699 --> 00:34:03,826
Please tell me
who the suspects are.

581
00:34:05,703 --> 00:34:06,746
No.

582
00:34:10,667 --> 00:34:12,043
Have it your way.

583
00:34:19,175 --> 00:34:21,135
You understand, I'm gonna
have to torture you.

584
00:34:35,281 --> 00:34:38,117
You don't seem like someone
who deals well with physical pain.

585
00:34:38,784 --> 00:34:40,161
Not particularly.

586
00:34:40,703 --> 00:34:44,206
- A name then.
- I can't do it.

587
00:34:46,792 --> 00:34:47,835
Fine.

588
00:34:59,221 --> 00:35:00,264
God!

589
00:35:02,725 --> 00:35:04,143
Okay, okay, okay.

590
00:35:06,270 --> 00:35:11,942
You... You... you have to understand
this is hard for me... emotionally.

591
00:35:13,277 --> 00:35:16,072
I've never said any of these
names out loud to anyone.

592
00:35:17,073 --> 00:35:18,157
A name.

593
00:35:19,492 --> 00:35:22,620
Turtmee.
First name... Don.

594
00:35:23,913 --> 00:35:25,414
Don Turtmee?

595
00:35:27,750 --> 00:35:29,126
"Don't hurt me."

596
00:35:31,587 --> 00:35:32,755
You got me.

597
00:35:33,297 --> 00:35:35,132
Why, I oughta...

598
00:35:35,633 --> 00:35:37,176
Put your hands in the air!

599
00:35:37,468 --> 00:35:40,179
Keep 'em where we can see 'em!
Jane, you all right?

600
00:35:40,304 --> 00:35:41,597
Oh, never better.

601
00:35:42,473 --> 00:35:44,141
I'm just fine. Dandy.

602
00:35:44,850 --> 00:35:47,269
Thanks to us. You were foolish
to come here alone.

603
00:35:54,902 --> 00:35:56,612
You taking pleasure in this?

604
00:35:58,572 --> 00:35:59,824
Don't get me wrong.

605
00:36:01,075 --> 00:36:04,578
- You are a pain in the ass.
- Well, thank you.

606
00:36:04,704 --> 00:36:07,081
But I wouldn't want anything
bad to happen to you

607
00:36:07,206 --> 00:36:08,958
especially when it would be my fault.

608
00:36:10,334 --> 00:36:12,378
Well, I'm sure your conscience
is clear now.

609
00:36:12,503 --> 00:36:15,131
It is. Now leave me alone.

610
00:36:15,548 --> 00:36:18,843
Next time I hear from you
you better be telling me Red John is dead.

611
00:36:19,343 --> 00:36:20,386
Deal.

612
00:36:21,846 --> 00:36:23,222
That is a deal.

613
00:36:25,558 --> 00:36:26,851
Thank you, Madeleine.

614
00:36:31,564 --> 00:36:32,940
You're welcome, Patrick.

615
00:36:34,358 --> 00:36:35,609
Good luck.

616
00:36:40,322 --> 00:36:41,407
Lisbon...

617
00:36:43,701 --> 00:36:45,161
- You good here?
- Yeah.

618
00:36:45,286 --> 00:36:46,871
You can call
for that backup now.

619
00:36:57,047 --> 00:36:58,466
You'll pay for this!

620
00:36:59,341 --> 00:37:01,719
I won't forget!
This is on you!

621
00:37:02,678 --> 00:37:03,846
You sure you're all right?

622
00:37:05,681 --> 00:37:09,560
The list is down to five names.
That is a very manageable number.

623
00:37:10,019 --> 00:37:11,854
Somehow that doesn't
reassure me.

624
00:37:13,856 --> 00:37:15,691
We're in the home stretch, Lisbon.

625
00:37:16,859 --> 00:37:20,196
Hey guys. So...

626
00:37:20,821 --> 00:37:22,531
looks like I got it
wrong about you.

627
00:37:22,656 --> 00:37:24,033
Yeah. Looks like you did.

628
00:37:24,158 --> 00:37:26,243
Ah, it's all right.
We all make mistakes.

629
00:37:27,620 --> 00:37:30,414
I mean, a...
a Homeland Security agent?

630
00:37:30,748 --> 00:37:32,082
Who would have thought, right?

631
00:37:33,459 --> 00:37:34,502
Not me.

632
00:37:35,753 --> 00:37:37,922
It's gonna be a jurisdictional
cluster-bomb.

633
00:37:39,215 --> 00:37:40,382
But we'll book him first
at county.

634
00:37:40,508 --> 00:37:43,219
In a few days he'll get transferred
to a federal detention center.

635
00:37:43,344 --> 00:37:46,722
- I'd like to talk to him for a sec.
- Sure. Hey, Jane.

636
00:37:49,850 --> 00:37:53,103
- No hard feelings, huh?
- No. No hard feelings.

637
00:37:55,314 --> 00:37:56,398
Lisbon.

638
00:38:02,613 --> 00:38:05,616
If you have anything else to say
now would be the time.

639
00:38:06,242 --> 00:38:10,913
Oh, I might know some things.
But this isn't the best place to talk.

640
00:38:12,248 --> 00:38:15,084
- Come see me sometime.
- I will.

641
00:38:16,961 --> 00:38:20,965
- Are you gonna finish this thing?
- Yes.

642
00:38:36,480 --> 00:38:39,567
- Hey.
- Hey. Lisbon called.

643
00:38:39,692 --> 00:38:41,318
They're not coming back
into the office tonight.

644
00:38:41,443 --> 00:38:43,445
- Okay.
- You can probably go, too.

645
00:38:43,571 --> 00:38:45,239
- There's nobody else here.
- Sure.

646
00:38:45,948 --> 00:38:48,576
- Did you change?
- What, this old thing?

647
00:38:50,160 --> 00:38:52,413
Wait. What's this?

648
00:38:56,375 --> 00:38:58,043
It looks like
a hotel room key.

649
00:38:59,211 --> 00:39:03,841
- Wait. You didn't, did you?
- I did. The honeymoon suite.

650
00:39:04,008 --> 00:39:05,509
Mrs. Capello
is watching Ben.

651
00:39:05,634 --> 00:39:07,970
That's amazing.
Thank you.

652
00:39:08,095 --> 00:39:10,514
- You know, I was beginning to think...
- I know what you thought.

653
00:39:11,098 --> 00:39:13,976
- And am I crazy?
- Yes.

654
00:39:15,311 --> 00:39:17,646
You shouldn't think
so much, Wayne.

655
00:39:21,817 --> 00:39:22,860
Come on.

656
00:39:48,928 --> 00:39:50,304
Don't mind me.

657
00:40:26,548 --> 00:40:27,800
Let me speak to him.

658
00:40:37,935 --> 00:40:39,728
What's going on?
What are you doing here?

659
00:40:40,354 --> 00:40:41,814
You hit the nail on the head, Bob.

660
00:40:42,648 --> 00:40:44,233
About what?

661
00:40:44,358 --> 00:40:46,902
There is a powerful organization
within law enforcement.

662
00:40:47,778 --> 00:40:49,405
But it's a lot bigger
than you thought.

663
00:40:50,114 --> 00:40:51,782
It's not just the big boys, either.

664
00:40:52,324 --> 00:40:55,035
They even let lower level
guys like me in.

665
00:40:55,411 --> 00:40:56,829
I know what you're doing.

666
00:40:57,788 --> 00:40:59,707
We've had our eye on you
for a long time.

667
00:41:00,916 --> 00:41:03,210
We just want to help.
It's what we do.

668
00:41:03,627 --> 00:41:04,962
Help me how?

669
00:41:07,423 --> 00:41:08,716
Listen to me closely.

670
00:41:09,675 --> 00:41:11,343
That's the address
of a safe house.

671
00:41:11,802 --> 00:41:13,429
It's 2 miles due east of here.

672
00:41:13,971 --> 00:41:16,265
When you get there,
someone will contact you

673
00:41:16,390 --> 00:41:18,726
get you some money,
I.D., clothes.

674
00:41:20,644 --> 00:41:21,854
I don't believe you.

675
00:41:27,484 --> 00:41:28,694
Come on, Bob.

676
00:41:29,111 --> 00:41:30,279
Get out of the van.

677
00:41:49,339 --> 00:41:52,051
- Start running.
- Just do it here.

678
00:41:52,968 --> 00:41:56,930
Actually, I'd prefer it if you Ran.
You know... Protocol.

679
00:42:21,538 --> 00:42:23,248
Agent Kirkland tried to escape.

680
00:42:23,749 --> 00:42:25,918
You had no choice
but to use deadly force.

681
00:42:26,043 --> 00:42:27,127
Do you understand?

682
00:42:28,420 --> 00:42:32,257
- Tiger tiger.
- Tiger tiger.


